u're gonna get you got it  a residential mortgage so a buy to let  mortgage typically they will lend you 75  percent to buy the house so again if  you're buying a house that's 200 000  pounds and you're buying it for the sole  intent to rent it out as a buy  two let then you're going to get  a 75 bucks mortgage which would be about  150 000 pounds  now here's the interesting part  let's say you buy it let's say you have  a 200 000 pound house  and you've got a mortgage outstanding of  150 000 pounds  you might be thinking oh that's debt pay  it down pay off but i would recommend  not thinking of it like that i would  recommend  leaving the debt  because the rent that you're getting  paid  should assuming that it's a good  property be paying the interest on the  debt and  paying you money which is your passive  income  so the debt's helping you that's what we  call it good debt so why pay off that  debt it's good it's helping you so you  keep the debt  now let's say the property this is where  refinancing comes in let's say the  property goes doubles in value over the  next 10 years let's say you buy it for  200 and then it becomes worth 400 000.  now  that two hundred thousand pounds uplift  the extra profit that you've got in the  house because it's gone up by two  hundred thousand that's your money right  but how do you get it  well you could sell the house  to get the money and then you'd have to  then wait if you sold the house you tell  it for 400 000  you'd get your 50 000 pounds deposit  back plus  bang you get your 200 000 pound uplift  but if you did that though you'd lose  the house  so how can you keep the house  but at the same time get your money  that's when you need to refinance or  remortgage because what you're going to  do the house is worth 200 000 is  refinance means  getting a new mortgage that's what it  means  so if you ever hear someone say oh i'm  refinancing what that means is they're  getting a new mortgage  if your house is worth 400 000  and you refinance it well how much is  the bank gonna lend you well they're  gonna lend you 75 percent  of 400 000 problem what about the  existing mortgage we'll get there so if  the house is now 400 000  and the new lender comes in and goes  well i'll give you 75 of the new value  therefore the new lender is prepared to  give you 300 000 here's what happens the  new lender gives you 300 000 but with  that 300 000 you have to pay the 150 000  so you pay off the old mortgage with the  new mortgage so if they give you a 300  000 mortgage you pay off the 150 000  pounds mortgage and you're left with 150  000 pounds so you got your money from  the refinance but you've still got the  house  your debt on the house has gone up but  so is the value  and now you've pulled the money out by  refinancing now that money how does it  work do they just give it to you does it  just go into your bank do you have to  pay tax on it how does it work yes they  just give it to you they give you that  money  into your bank and you pay zero tax on  that money that is a loan the reason you  pay no tax on the money is because even  though it's technically profit because  your house has gone up in value so  you've gained  and then you've taken the money out  you've taken it out in the form of debt  you've taken it out in the form of a  loan and they the hmrc the government  cannot tax you on debt it's a very  clever way to release money out of a  property without selling the property  because if you sell the property you're  going to pay capital gains tax you're  also going to lose the property now  you've got the money it's tax-free in  your bank you can use that money to buy  more houses and you've still got the  rent coming in so that's why refinancing  is so good now here's some complications  that i need to give you about  refinancing  when you buy a house  you can't refinance it for usually two  to three years and the reason is is  because  let's say you buy a house really cheap  and then you add value and the house  just shoots up in value in the next 12  months you buy it for 100 grand and  after six months it's worth 150. you  can't refinance it because the first  lender  the mortgage company that gave you that  first mortgage will usually tie you into  at least two years of a fixed term  so if after six months you want to get a  new mortgage and pay that old blender  off they'll be like  you've only you've only had the money  six months so you need to pay us  something called early repayment fees  which is usually  a few thousand pounds so if you're  buying a property with the sole purpose  of pushing the value up  and then refinancing it and pulling your  money out you shouldn't buy it with a  mortgage in the first instance you  should buy it with a bridging loan  because a bridging loan is something  that bridges the gap between the  purchase and the refinance i hope this  is making sense when i first learned  this about 14 years ago it blew my mind  i didn't understand it so maybe you have  to watch this video a couple times over  um but if you can get your head around  this it will really help you as a  property investor next next thing maybe  you bought a house and you live in the  house and it's a residential house maybe  you bought the house for 150 grand maybe  it's a flat but it's your home  if your home doubles in value let's say  you bought it for 150 now it's worth 300  can you refinance it and the answer is  yes you can refinance it on a  residential mortgage the only thing to  be mindful of  is that if you're refinancing your home  the new mortgage lender will want to  make sure that you can afford the new  mortgage payments so if you're earning  really low but you've got a house that's  really high in value and you're trying  to get a mortgage on it you might  struggle you might be able to just sell  it and then use the money to reinvest in  property so there's a little bit of a  crash course on refinancing and what it  means i hope you found this video  helpful i'm going to be making more  videos similar to this so hit me with  your questions below and i'll see you  next time  [Music]  you

